---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';

// What LoopSkill does and does not do — the honest-scope page.
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. Adam, 2026-08-07: the customer-facing site needed
// reworking because "the pricing is misleading, and documentation so the
// potential user knows what to expect". A marketplace that oversells its
// capabilities buys one signup and loses the user permanently on day two.
//
// THE RULE FOR EDITING THIS PAGE: every row below must be backed by something
// you can RUN. If a capability's evidence is a plan, a PR, or an intention, it
// belongs in "Not built" — not in "Works today". When you ship something, move
// the row AND name the probe that proves it. Never soften a "Not built" row
// into a hedge; a hedge is still a claim.
//
// Evidence for the current state was verified live on 2026-08-13 (previously
// 2026-08-07 — see issue #58, the install-count proof had gone stale: 223 →
// 490 recorded installs in the same window).

const works = [
  {
    title: 'Browse and install skills from any agent',
    body: 'Search the public catalog and install a skill into Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenClaw, Hermes, or anything else that speaks MCP. Installation is a pull: your agent fetches the skill. Nothing is pushed to your machine.',
    proof: '492 recorded installs in production (verified 2026-08-13 via /api/stats).',
  },
  {
    title: 'Run a loop and get an objective pass/fail',
    body: 'A loop executes under enforced bounds and returns a real exit code and duration — not a model\'s opinion that it went well. This is the part no other skill marketplace does.',
    proof: 'Live: run_id, passed, exit_code, duration_seconds returned per run.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Keep separate clients cryptographically isolated',
    body: 'Each client is its own organisation. A credential minted for one org is rejected against another, and the org is derived from the signature — never from a field in the request. So a misconfigured request cannot cross the boundary; it fails.',
    proof: 'Two independent paths, both probed against production with a same-org control that must pass first. Mesh credentials: 403 cross_tenant_rejected in both directions. Bundle reads and reconcile: 404 in both directions — indistinguishable from a bundle that does not exist, because a distinct forbidden code would confirm the id is real.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Route a client agent\'s defect into your private repo',
    body: 'When an agent you operate hits a problem with a skill from your bundle, the report lands as an issue in a private repository you control — not in a public tracker, and not in your inbox.',
    proof: 'Private defect routing proven: a report from a client agent lands as an issue in the private sink, verified at GitHub, and the sink is confirmed private by an anonymous request returning 404. The patch-and-redeploy half of the loop has shipped but has not yet been run end to end.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Self-host the whole thing',
    body: 'MPL-2.0. Clone it, docker compose up, and you have the registry, the runner and the catalog on your own hardware. Nothing phones home. Pro is hosting convenience, never a feature gate.',
    proof: 'Cold clone verified: healthy container, seeded catalog, README demo runs verbatim.',
  },
];

const partial = [
  {
    title: 'Fleet convergence reporting',
    body: 'You can see which loop assignments have converged, which are failing, and which have never run. What it does NOT yet do is age a never-run assignment into an alarm — a loop assigned and never fired looks healthy at the top level.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Connector catalog',
    body: 'Thousands of MCP servers are staged behind a review gate. The public catalog is deliberately empty until a human promotes an entry, so you will not find them by searching yet. Staging is not publishing.',
  },
];

const notBuilt = [
  {
    title: 'Pushing a bundle to a machine',
    body: 'LoopSkill never initiates a connection to your agent or your client\'s agent. Delivery is pull-only: the agent reconciles itself against the bundle. If you read "deploy to a client" anywhere and understood it as a push, that is not what happens.',
  },
  {
    title: 'A hosted dashboard for your clients',
    body: 'There is no client-facing login, seat, or portal. LoopSkill is a tool for the operator; your clients never touch it. That is a deliberate design decision, not a gap we are racing to close.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Usage-based or per-agent billing',
    body: 'Pricing is a flat subscription. We do not meter agents, runs, or seats, and we have no plans to — skills are plain text and metering them would be theatre.',
  },
];
---

<AppShell
  mode="public"
  title="What LoopSkill does & does not do — LoopSkill Docs"
  description="An honest scope page: what is proven and running today, what is partial, and what is not built. Read this before planning around a capability."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow text-accent mb-3">Documentation</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-5">
      What LoopSkill does — and what it doesn't
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Most tools tell you what they will eventually do. This page tells you what
      runs today, so you can decide in five minutes whether LoopSkill fits — and
      leave without wasting an afternoon if it doesn't.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft leading-relaxed mb-12">
      Every claim in the first section is backed by something we can run on
      demand. Anything we cannot demonstrate is in the last section, stated
      plainly rather than described as coming soon.
    </p>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-1">Works today</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft mb-6">Verified 2026-08-07.</p>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
        {works.map(item => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-card p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="text-accent mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-text mb-1.5">{item.title}</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{item.body}</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-muted-soft mt-2.5 font-mono">{item.proof}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-1">Partially built</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft mb-6">
        Real, in production, and honestly incomplete. Plan around the limits, not the label.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
        {partial.map(item => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-card p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="text-muted mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-xs" aria-hidden="true">◑</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-text mb-1.5">{item.title}</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{item.body}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-1">Not built</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft mb-6">
        If your plan depends on one of these, LoopSkill is not the right tool today.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
        {notBuilt.map(item => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-card p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="text-muted-soft mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">✕</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-text mb-1.5">{item.title}</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{item.body}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-xl border border-accent/30 bg-accent/5 p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-text mb-2">Who this is for</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        People who run AI agents for more than one client — independent
        consultants and small shops. Every agent framework assumes one operator
        and one environment; if you have five clients, you have five sets of
        credentials, five configurations, and no way to keep them from touching
        each other. That is the gap LoopSkill fills.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        If you run agents for exactly one organisation — your own — you probably
        do not need the isolation, and the free self-host will serve you fine.
        We would rather tell you that now than take $9.95 a month for it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="text-xs text-muted-soft mt-10">
      Something here inaccurate? That is a defect, not a nitpick — tell us and we
      will correct it. Last reviewed 2026-08-07.
    </p>
  </div>
</AppShell>
